PUT /products
{
  "settings": {
    "number_of_shards": 3,
    "number_of_replicas": 1,
    "analysis": {
      "analyzer": {
        "product_analyzer": {
          "type": "custom",
          "tokenizer": "standard",
          "filter": ["lowercase", "stop", "snowball"]
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "id": { "type": "long" },
      "name": {
        "type": "text",
        "analyzer": "product_analyzer",
        "fields": {
          "keyword": { "type": "keyword", "ignore_above": 256 }
        }
      },
      "description": { "type": "text", "analyzer": "product_analyzer" },
      "category": { "type": "keyword" },
      "price": { "type": "double" },
      "stock": { "type": "integer" },
      "tags": { "type": "keyword" },
      "created_at": { "type": "date", "format": "yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ssZ" },
      "attributes": { "type": "nested" }
    }
  }
}

Search products by name (text) AND category (keyword) AND price range, ranked by relevance:

POST /products/_search
{
  "query": {
    "bool": {
      "must": [
        { "match": { "name": "running shoes" } }
      ],
      "filter": [
        { "term": { "category": "footwear" } },
        { "range": { "price": { "gte": 50, "lte": 200 } } },
        { "term": { "in_stock": true } }
      ]
    }
  }
}

Note: name uses match (text field, analyzed). category and in_stock use term (keyword/boolean, exact). price uses range. All non-scoring conditions are in filter context for caching.

Annotate your entity with @Document(indexName = 'products') and map fields with @Field. Use FieldType.Text with an analyzer for full-text fields, FieldType.Keyword for exact-match fields, and multi-fields for both:

@Document(indexName = 'products')
public class ProductDocument {
    @Id private String id;
    @Field(type = FieldType.Text, analyzer = 'standard') private String name;
    @Field(type = FieldType.Keyword) private String category;
    @Field(type = FieldType.Double) private double price;
    @Field(type = FieldType.Date, format = DateFormat.date_hour_minute_second) private LocalDateTime createdAt;
}

Extend ElasticsearchRepository for CRUD. Add derived query methods for simple searches. Use @Query with a JSON query string for complex queries:

public interface ProductSearchRepository extends ElasticsearchRepository<ProductDocument, String> {
    List<ProductDocument> findByCategory(String category);
    List<ProductDocument> findByPriceBetween(double min, double max);
    @Query("{\"bool\": {\"must\": [{\"match\": {\"name\": \"?0\"}}], \"filter\": [{\"term\": {\"category\": \"?1\"}}]}}") List<ProductDocument> searchByNameAndCategory(String name, String category);
}

For full query DSL control, inject ElasticsearchOperations and build queries programmatically:

@Service
public class ProductSearchService {
    private final ElasticsearchOperations ops;

    public SearchHits<ProductDocument> search(String query, String category, double maxPrice) {
        Query q = NativeQuery.builder()
            .withQuery(qb -> qb.bool(b -> b
                .must(m -> m.match(mq -> mq.field("name").query(query)))
                .filter(f -> f.term(t -> t.field("category").value(category)))
                .filter(f -> f.range(r -> r.field("price").lte(JsonData.of(maxPrice))))
            ))
            .withPageable(PageRequest.of(0, 20))
            .build();
        return ops.search(q, ProductDocument.class);
    }
}

// Example corpus (3 documents)
// Doc 1: "Spring Boot makes Java easy"
// Doc 2: "Java is fast and easy"
// Doc 3: "Spring makes apps easy"

// Inverted index built after analysis (lowercase, stop word removal):
{
  "spring":  { "postings": [{ "doc": 1, "freq": 1 }, { "doc": 3, "freq": 1 }] },
  "boot":    { "postings": [{ "doc": 1, "freq": 1 }] },
  "java":    { "postings": [{ "doc": 1, "freq": 1 }, { "doc": 2, "freq": 1 }] },
  "easy":    { "postings": [{ "doc": 1, "freq": 1 }, { "doc": 2, "freq": 1 }, { "doc": 3, "freq": 1 }] },
  "fast":    { "postings": [{ "doc": 2, "freq": 1 }] },
  "apps":    { "postings": [{ "doc": 3, "freq": 1 }] }
}

// Query: "spring java"
// Look up "spring" -> docs [1, 3]
// Look up "java"   -> docs [1, 2]
// AND -> doc 1 (contains both terms) scores highest

// BM25 scoring factors:
// TF (term frequency): how many times the term appears in the document
// IDF (inverse document frequency): log(N / df) where N = total docs, df = docs with term
// "easy" appears in all 3 docs -> low IDF -> low importance
// "boot" appears in only 1 doc  -> high IDF -> high importance for that query

// SQL LIKE comparison -- why it is slow
// SELECT * FROM articles WHERE body LIKE '%java%'
// Must scan EVERY row; no index structure helps for mid-string matches

// Problematic: from/size deep pagination
POST /products/_search
{
  "from": 5000,
  "size": 10,
  "sort": [{ "_score": "desc" }, { "created_at": "desc" }],
  "query": { "match": { "name": "running shoes" } }
}

// What Elasticsearch actually does internally:
// Each of 3 shards computes and returns 5010 hits
// Coordinating node receives 3 * 5010 = 15,030 hits
// Sorts all 15,030, discards first 5000, returns 10
// Cost grows linearly with 'from' -- page 5000 is 500x more expensive than page 1

// Solution: search_after cursor-based pagination (recommended)
// First page -- no search_after, save the last hit's sort values
POST /products/_search
{
  "size": 10,
  "sort": [{ "_score": "desc" }, { "created_at": "desc" }, { "_id": "asc" }],
  "query": { "match": { "name": "running shoes" } }
}
// Response: last hit sort values = [0.87, "2024-01-15T10:00:00Z", "abc123"]

// Next page -- pass last hit's sort values as search_after
POST /products/_search
{
  "size": 10,
  "sort": [{ "_score": "desc" }, { "created_at": "desc" }, { "_id": "asc" }],
  "search_after": [0.87, "2024-01-15T10:00:00Z", "abc123"],
  "query": { "match": { "name": "running shoes" } }
}
// Each shard only needs to return 10 results after the cursor -- O(1) cost at any depth
// Note: always include a tiebreaker (_id) to make sort order deterministic

// For bulk export, use the Point In Time (PIT) API with search_after
// to get a consistent snapshot across multiple requests:
POST /products/_pit?keep_alive=1m
// Use the returned pit.id with search_after requests

// Bucket + metric nested aggregation
// Get top 5 categories, with average price and total count in each
POST /products/_search
{
  "size": 0,
  "query": {
    "bool": {
      "filter": [{ "range": { "price": { "lte": 500 } } }]
    }
  },
  "aggs": {
    "by_category": {
      "terms": {
        "field": "category",
        "size": 5
      },
      "aggs": {
        "avg_price": {
          "avg": { "field": "price" }
        },
        "price_ranges": {
          "range": {
            "field": "price",
            "ranges": [
              { "to": 50 },
              { "from": 50, "to": 200 },
              { "from": 200 }
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "monthly_sales": {
      "date_histogram": {
        "field": "created_at",
        "calendar_interval": "month"
      },
      "aggs": {
        "total_revenue": {
          "sum": { "field": "price" }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// Java with Spring Data Elasticsearch
NativeQuery query = NativeQuery.builder()
    .withQuery(q -> q.matchAll(m -> m))
    .withAggregation("by_category",
        Aggregation.of(a -> a
            .terms(t -> t.field("category").size(5))))
    .build();
SearchHits<ProductDocument> hits = ops.search(query, ProductDocument.class);
ElasticsearchAggregations aggregations = (ElasticsearchAggregations) hits.getAggregations();

Elasticsearch aggregations power real-time analytics without pre-computed rollup tables. Understanding how to compose aggregations is essential for building dashboards over large datasets.

**Bucket + metric aggregation pattern.** Most analytics queries combine bucket aggregations (grouping documents) with metric aggregations (computing stats within each bucket). For example, a sales dashboard might group orders by product category (terms bucket), then compute average order value and total revenue within each category (avg and sum metric aggregations). Nesting is the key — metric aggregations are children of bucket aggregations.

```json
{
  "size": 0,
  "aggs": {
    "by_category": {
      "terms": { "field": "category.keyword", "size": 20 },
      "aggs": {
        "avg_price": { "avg": { "field": "price" } },
        "total_revenue": { "sum": { "field": "price" } },
        "monthly_trend": {
          "date_histogram": {
            "field": "order_date",
            "calendar_interval": "month"
          },
          "aggs": {
            "revenue": { "sum": { "field": "price" } }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

**Performance considerations.** Set `size: 0` on the top-level query when you only need aggregation results, not individual documents. Use `shard_size` on terms aggregations to control accuracy vs performance — the default is `size * 1.5 + 10` per shard, which works for most cases but can be increased for high-cardinality fields where accuracy matters. For dashboards hitting Elasticsearch every few seconds, use the `filter` aggregation to scope results to the relevant time window rather than filtering at the query level, which allows Elasticsearch to cache aggregation results more effectively.

**Pipeline aggregations** compute values derived from other aggregations. The `derivative` pipeline aggregation calculates period-over-period change (e.g., month-over-month revenue growth). The `bucket_selector` filters buckets based on computed metrics (e.g., only show categories with revenue > $10,000). These enable SQL-like `HAVING` clauses and window functions within the aggregation framework.

The simplest approach: write to PostgreSQL, then immediately index to Elasticsearch in the same service method. Fast to implement but fragile -- if the Elasticsearch write fails after the DB commit, the search index is silently stale. Mitigation: wrap the Elasticsearch write in a retry with exponential backoff, and alert on persistent failures. This is acceptable for low-risk data (e.g., product descriptions) but not for business-critical search (e.g., order status).

@Transactional
public void saveProduct(Product product) {
    productRepository.save(product);  // PostgreSQL commit
    // Elasticsearch write outside the transaction
    searchRepository.save(toDocument(product));
}

After the DB write commits, publish a ProductUpdated event to Kafka. A separate consumer service reads the event and indexes to Elasticsearch. Benefits: the application is decoupled from Elasticsearch availability; Kafka's at-least-once delivery ensures events are not lost; consumers can retry failed indexing without impacting the API. Downside: requires a message broker and introduces latency between write and search visibility.

@Transactional
public void saveProduct(Product product) {
    productRepository.save(product);
    kafkaTemplate.send("product-events", new ProductUpdatedEvent(product));
}

// Separate consumer
@KafkaListener(topics = "product-events")
public void onProductUpdated(ProductUpdatedEvent event) {
    searchRepository.save(toDocument(event.getProduct()));
}

