// Custom analyzer for autocomplete (edge_ngram)
PUT /products
{
  "settings": {
    "analysis": {
      "analyzer": {
        "autocomplete_index": {
          "tokenizer": "autocomplete_tokenizer",
          "filter": ["lowercase"]
        },
        "autocomplete_query": {
          "tokenizer": "standard",
          "filter": ["lowercase"]
        }
      },
      "tokenizer": {
        "autocomplete_tokenizer": {
          "type": "edge_ngram",
          "min_gram": 2,
          "max_gram": 20,
          "token_chars": ["letter", "digit"]
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "name": {
        "type": "text",
        "analyzer": "autocomplete_index",
        "search_analyzer": "autocomplete_query"
      }
    }
  }
}
// Index time: 'boot' -> [bo, boo, boot]
// Query time: 'boo' -> [boo] -> matches documents with 'boot'
// search_analyzer overrides analyzer for queries only

Out-of-the-box analyzers handle basic use cases, but production search features almost always need custom analyzer configuration. Understanding the analyzer pipeline (character filters → tokenizer → token filters) lets you build search experiences that match user expectations.

**Autocomplete with edge_ngram.** Users expect results as they type. The `edge_ngram` token filter generates prefix tokens: "spring" becomes ["s", "sp", "spr", "spri", "sprin", "spring"]. Configure `min_gram: 2` and `max_gram: 10` to avoid single-character tokens (too noisy) and very long tokens (wasteful). Use this analyzer only at index time — the search analyzer should use `standard` so the user's complete query matches against the indexed prefixes.

```json
{
  "settings": {
    "analysis": {
      "analyzer": {
        "autocomplete_index": {
          "type": "custom",
          "tokenizer": "standard",
          "filter": ["lowercase", "edge_ngram_filter"]
        },
        "autocomplete_search": {
          "type": "custom",
          "tokenizer": "standard",
          "filter": ["lowercase"]
        }
      },
      "filter": {
        "edge_ngram_filter": {
          "type": "edge_ngram",
          "min_gram": 2,
          "max_gram": 10
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

**Synonym handling.** Product searches often need synonyms: "laptop" should match "notebook", "tv" should match "television". Use a `synonym` token filter with an external synonym file that can be updated without reindexing. Apply synonyms at search time only (via `search_analyzer`) so the index stays clean and synonym changes take effect immediately.

**Language-specific analysis.** For multilingual content, use language-specific analyzers (e.g., `german`, `french`) that handle stemming, stop words, and compound word decomposition correctly. Map each language field separately (`title_en`, `title_de`) with its own analyzer, and use a `multi_match` query across all language fields at search time.
